[580.8445, "o", "Done. `/app/model.xml` is written and passes both tests.\r\n\r\n## What I changed\r\n\r\nOnly the solver configuration in a new `<option>` tag — no physical properties, geometry, masses, joints, plugins, or timestep were touched:\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n<option solver=\"PGS\" jacobian=\"dense\" tolerance=\"1e-12\"/>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Why this works\r\n\r\nProfiling showed the step time is dominated by `mj_fwdConstraint`, not by the cable plugin or kinematics:\r\n\r\n| Setting | Time (2 s sim) | Final-state error vs ref |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Reference (Newton, auto Jacobian, tol 1e-8) | ~268 ms | — |\r\n| PGS, auto Jacobian | ~132 ms | ~4e-6 |\r\n| **PGS, dense Jacobian, tol 1e-12** | **~123 ms** | **~6e-8** |\r\n\r\n- **PGS instead of Newton**: the model has only 3 constraint equations (the single `connect` equality), so the Newton solver's overhead buys nothing. PGS is ~2× faster.\r\n- **Dense Jacobian**: `auto` was selecting the sparse path here, which is slower for this small, near-chain system; forcing `dense` saves another ~10%.\r\n- **Tighter tolerance "]
[580.844587, "o", "`1e-12`**: recovers essentially exact dynamics (worst error ~6e-8 over 200 random seeds, vs the `1e-5` requirement) while staying fast. The timestep remains `0.002`, so the state is still sampled at exactly `t = 2.0 s`.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\n\r\n- `python eval.py` (run 4×): **time pctg 0.46**, speedup ~2.17×, final state difference 0.0000.\r\n- Extra sweep over 200 random seeds: worst absolute state difference **5.7e-8**, zero failures, no NaN/Inf.\r\n- `diff model_ref.xml model.xml` shows only the added `<option>` line.\r\n"]
[580.944636, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@d749a8c25e4e:/app# "]
